index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..51aa51d4a87b4cdb1552ff354a76a7ad74fa8200
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/memory/decision-log/dl-004-createpatch-array-diff-index-aligned-no-move-lcs-detection.md
@@ -0,0 +1,49 @@
+---
+id: dl-004-createpatch-array-diff-index-aligned-no-move-lcs-detection
+type: decision-log
+title: "createPatch array diff: index-aligned, no move/LCS detection"
+status: pending
+---
+
+## Context
+
+`createPatch(from, to)` (task-005) needs a strategy for diffing two arrays at the same path. RFC
+6902 does not prescribe one: it defines what `add`/`remove`/`replace`/`move`/`copy` do once chosen,
+not how a diff should choose between them. Two broad options existed:
+
+1. **Index-aligned diff**: compare `from[i]` to `to[i]` for the common length, then append or
+   remove the trailing elements of whichever side is longer. Simple, and already matches how
+   `diffObjects` treats members: no attempt to detect that an element merely moved.
+2. **Content-aware diff** (e.g. an LCS-based alignment, as `diff`/`fast-json-patch` use, possibly
+   emitting `move`): finds the smallest edit script for insertions/deletions/moves anywhere in the
+   array, not only at the end.
+
+Option 2 produces a strictly smaller patch when an element is inserted, removed, or reordered in
+the middle of an array — the exact case this task's "describe only what changed" requirement cares
+about. But it is a materially larger algorithm (an LCS or similar alignment, plus matching rules
+for *which* values count as "the same element" when they are objects that also changed slightly),
+and it is not needed for this library's actual use case so far: configuration files, API payloads
+and stored records, where `README.md`'s examples (and task-005's own) show arrays mostly growing or
+shrinking at the end (e.g. `tags`), not being reordered.
+
+## Decision
+
+`createPatch` aligns array elements by index (see `diffArrays` in `src/index.ts`): it diffs the
+common-length prefix recursively, then emits a trailing `add` (at `path/-`) for each extra element
+if `to` is longer, or a trailing `remove` (highest index first) for each extra element if `from` is
+longer. No `move` is ever emitted by `createPatch`, and no attempt is made to detect that an
+element was inserted or removed before the end of the array.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- A pure trailing append or removal — the common case this project's own documents exhibit — still
+  produces a minimal, single-operation patch per element.
+- An insertion, removal, or reorder in the *middle* of an array produces a patch with one
+  `replace` per shifted index from that point to the end of the common length, plus a trailing
+  `add`/`remove`, rather than the single `add`/`remove`/`move` a content-aware diff would produce.
+  This is correct (`applyPatch(from, createPatch(from, to))` always deep-equals `to`, and is
+  covered by a round-trip assertion in every `createPatch` test) but not minimal in that case.
+- If a client's real workload turns out to reorder or insert into the middle of large arrays
+  often enough for this to matter, revisit with a content-aware (LCS/move-aware) array diff then;
+  doing so now would be speculative complexity against an access pattern this project does not yet
+  have evidence of.
