+++ b/docs/memory/task/task-005-implement-json-patch-rfc-6902-createpatch-diff.md
@@ -0,0 +1,62 @@
+---
+id: task-005-implement-json-patch-rfc-6902-createpatch-diff
+type: task
+title: "Implement JSON Patch (RFC 6902) createPatch (diff)"
+status: pending
+---
+
+## Request
+
+Clients want to send only what changed between two versions of a JSON document, instead of the
+whole new version. Add `createPatch(from, to)`, exported from `src/index.ts`, that returns a JSON
+Patch (RFC 6902) which turns `from` into `to` when applied, without mutating either argument. The
+patch must describe only what changed, not the whole document, for small changes.
+
+## What was done
+
+Added `createPatch(from, to)` to `src/index.ts`, built on the `isObject`, `deepClone` and
+`deepEqual` helpers already used by `applyPatch`/`applyMergePatch`. It is a structural diff: a
+private `diffValues(path, from, to, patch)` walks `from` and `to` together —
+
+- if the two values are deep-equal (per the existing `deepEqual`), nothing is emitted;
+- if both are objects, `diffObjects` emits a `remove` for each member only `from` has, an `add`
+  (with a deep-cloned value) for each member only `to` has, and recurses into members both sides
+  have, at that member's own path;
+- if both are arrays, `diffArrays` recurses index-by-index over the common length, then appends
+  (`add` at `path/-`) `to`'s extra trailing elements if it is longer, or removes (`remove`)
+  `from`'s extra trailing elements, highest index first, if it is shorter;
+- otherwise (a JSON type change at this path, or two different scalars) it emits a single
+  `replace` at `path` with a deep-cloned `to`. This also covers the whole-document case
+  (`path: ""`), reusing the `replace`-at-root behaviour decided in dl-002.
+
+A new `escapeToken`/`childPath` pair builds each child path, escaping `~` and `/` in object member
+names (`~` → `~0` first, then `/` → `~1`, the exact inverse of `parsePointer`'s unescaping order),
+so a patch produced by `createPatch` always resolves correctly through `resolvePointer`/
+`applyPatch` even when a member name itself contains JSON Pointer special characters.
+
+Followed the project's test-first directive: added tests to `src/index.test.ts` and confirmed red
+(`createPatch` did not exist) before implementing. Added 13 new cases, all new behaviour: an
+empty patch for equal documents, add/remove/replace of a single member, recursion into a nested
+object, trailing array append/remove, a combined in-place-change-plus-trailing-removal case,
+whole-document replace on a type change, escaping of `~`/`/` in member names, non-mutation of
+`from`/`to`, and non-aliasing of an inserted value with `to`. Every case except the last two
+(mutation/aliasing) also asserts the actual contract — `applyPatch(from, createPatch(from, to))`
+deep-equals `to` — via a small `assertTurnsFromIntoTo` helper, since the computed shape of the
+patch is secondary to that round trip. `node --test`: 73/73 (60 pre-existing + 13 new).
+
+Verified `src/index.ts` (the file actually published, per `dist`) still compiles with zero errors
+under the existing strict `tsconfig.json`, using a `typescript` install already cached locally from
+an earlier task's temporary install (not added as a dependency, consistent with dl-001).
+
+Documented `createPatch` in `README.md`, directly under `applyPatch`, since both belong to the
+same RFC 6902 feature.
+
+Recorded the array-diffing strategy as a decision-log (see below), since it is a real algorithmic
+choice with a user-visible consequence (patch size/shape for array edits other than a pure
+trailing append/removal) rather than something RFC 6902 dictates.
+
+## Follow-ups
+
+- dl-001's decision to defer `tsc` typechecking of test files still applies; this change was
+  verified the same way.
+- See the new decision-log for the array-diffing trade-off and why it was accepted as-is for now.
